WEBVTT

00:00.690 --> 00:03.446
I've drawn a crazy looking
function here in yellow.

00:03.446 --> 00:04.820
And what I want
to think about is

00:04.820 --> 00:07.370
when this function takes
on the maximum values

00:07.370 --> 00:08.674
and minimum values.

00:08.674 --> 00:10.090
And for the sake
of this video, we

00:10.090 --> 00:12.130
can assume that the
graph of this function

00:12.130 --> 00:14.410
just keeps getting lower
and lower and lower

00:14.410 --> 00:16.900
as x becomes more and more
negative, and lower and lower

00:16.900 --> 00:19.430
and lower as x goes
beyond the interval

00:19.430 --> 00:21.430
that I've depicted
right over here.

00:21.430 --> 00:24.580
So what is the maximum value
that this function takes on?

00:24.580 --> 00:25.780
Well we can eyeball that.

00:25.780 --> 00:30.030
It looks like it's at that
point right over there.

00:30.030 --> 00:36.390
So we would call this
a global maximum.

00:36.390 --> 00:36.890
the?

00:36.890 --> 00:40.020
Function never takes on
a value larger than this.

00:40.020 --> 00:46.450
So we could say that we have a
global maximum at the point x0.

00:46.450 --> 00:52.900
Because f of of x0 is
greater than, or equal to,

00:52.900 --> 00:56.350
f of x, for any other
x in the domain.

00:56.350 --> 00:59.140
And that's pretty obvious,
when you look at it like this.

00:59.140 --> 01:01.070
Now do we have a
global minimum point,

01:01.070 --> 01:02.650
the way that I've drawn it?

01:02.650 --> 01:03.310
Well, no.

01:03.310 --> 01:08.070
This function can take an
arbitrarily negative values.

01:08.070 --> 01:09.672
It approaches
negative infinity as x

01:09.672 --> 01:10.880
approaches negative infinity.

01:10.880 --> 01:12.420
It approaches
negative infinity as x

01:12.420 --> 01:14.080
approaches positive infinity.

01:14.080 --> 01:16.100
So we have-- let me
write this down--

01:16.100 --> 01:24.190
we have no global minimum.

01:24.190 --> 01:25.690
Now let me ask you a question.

01:25.690 --> 01:29.630
Do we have local
minima or local maxima?

01:29.630 --> 01:32.370
When I say minima, it's
just the plural of minimum.

01:32.370 --> 01:35.830
And maxima is just
the plural of maximum.

01:35.830 --> 01:40.430
So do we have a local minima
here, or local minimum here?

01:40.430 --> 01:43.300
Well, a local minimum,
you could imagine means

01:43.300 --> 01:45.180
that that value of the
function at that point

01:45.180 --> 01:47.284
is lower than the
points around it.

01:47.284 --> 01:49.700
So right over here, it looks
like we have a local minimum.

01:54.310 --> 01:56.800
And I'm not giving a very
rigorous definition here.

01:56.800 --> 01:58.330
But one way to
think about it is,

01:58.330 --> 02:02.940
we can say that we have a
local minimum point at x1,

02:02.940 --> 02:06.690
as if we have a region
around x1, where f of x1

02:06.690 --> 02:10.910
is less than an f of x for any x
in this region right over here.

02:10.910 --> 02:13.060
And it's pretty easy
to eyeball, too.

02:13.060 --> 02:16.950
This is a low point for any
of the values of f around it,

02:16.950 --> 02:18.540
right over there.

02:18.540 --> 02:21.000
Now do we have any
other local minima?

02:21.000 --> 02:22.570
Well it doesn't look like we do.

02:22.570 --> 02:25.360
Now what about local maxima?

02:25.360 --> 02:31.275
Well this one right over
here-- let me do it in purple,

02:31.275 --> 02:33.150
I don't want to get
people confused, actually

02:33.150 --> 02:35.670
let me do it in this color--
this point right over here

02:35.670 --> 02:38.590
looks like a local maximum.

02:38.590 --> 02:40.620
Not lox, that would have
to deal with salmon.

02:40.620 --> 02:46.960
Local maximum, right over there.

02:46.960 --> 02:53.790
So we could say at the point
x1, or sorry, at the point x2,

02:53.790 --> 02:56.890
we have a local
maximum point at x2.

02:56.890 --> 03:01.210
Because f of x2 is larger
than f of x for any

03:01.210 --> 03:03.814
x around a
neighborhood around x2.

03:03.814 --> 03:04.980
I'm not being very rigorous.

03:04.980 --> 03:07.280
But you can see it
just by looking at it.

03:07.280 --> 03:08.260
So that's fair enough.

03:08.260 --> 03:12.260
We've identified all of the
maxima and minima, often called

03:12.260 --> 03:15.630
the extrema, for this function.

03:15.630 --> 03:18.270
Now how can we identify
those, if we knew something

03:18.270 --> 03:20.434
about the derivative
of the function?

03:20.434 --> 03:21.850
Well, let's look
at the derivative

03:21.850 --> 03:25.390
at each of these points.

03:25.390 --> 03:27.370
So at this first
point, right over here,

03:27.370 --> 03:30.220
if I were to try to
visualize the tangent line--

03:30.220 --> 03:32.570
let me do that in a
better color than brown.

03:32.570 --> 03:35.240
If I were to try to
visualize the tangent line,

03:35.240 --> 03:37.130
it would look
something like that.

03:37.130 --> 03:38.890
So the slope here is 0.

03:38.890 --> 03:43.180
So we would say that f
prime of x0 is equal to 0.

03:43.180 --> 03:45.800
The slope of the tangent
line at this point is 0.

03:45.800 --> 03:47.140
What about over here?

03:47.140 --> 03:49.970
Well, once again,
the tangent line

03:49.970 --> 03:52.400
would look something like that.

03:52.400 --> 03:59.390
So once again, we would say
f prime at x1 is equal to 0.

03:59.390 --> 04:01.030
What about over here?

04:01.030 --> 04:04.910
Well, here the tangent line
is actually not well defined.

04:04.910 --> 04:06.705
We have a positive
slope going into it,

04:06.705 --> 04:09.180
and then it immediately jumps
to being a negative slope.

04:09.180 --> 04:14.034
So over here, f prime
of x2 is not defined.

04:14.034 --> 04:15.200
Let me just write undefined.

04:18.517 --> 04:21.100
So we have an interesting-- and
once again, I'm not rigorously

04:21.100 --> 04:24.100
proving it to you, I just want
you to get the intuition here.

04:24.100 --> 04:27.870
We see that if we have
some type of an extrema--

04:27.870 --> 04:29.820
and we're not
talking about when x

04:29.820 --> 04:31.497
is at an endpoint
of an interval,

04:31.497 --> 04:33.830
just to be clear what I'm
talking about when I'm talking

04:33.830 --> 04:36.032
about x as an endpoint
of an interval.

04:36.032 --> 04:37.740
We're saying, let's
say that the function

04:37.740 --> 04:42.905
is where you have an
interval from there.

04:42.905 --> 04:45.280
So let's say a function starts
right over there, and then

04:45.280 --> 04:45.871
keeps going.

04:45.871 --> 04:48.370
This would be a maximum point,
but it would be an end point.

04:48.370 --> 04:50.203
We're not talking about
endpoints right now.

04:50.203 --> 04:52.960
We're talking about when
we have points in between,

04:52.960 --> 04:56.000
or when our interval
is infinite.

04:56.000 --> 04:59.540
So we're not talking
about points like that,

04:59.540 --> 05:00.866
or points like this.

05:00.866 --> 05:02.615
We're talking about
the points in between.

05:05.690 --> 05:09.040
So if you have a point
inside of an interval,

05:09.040 --> 05:10.720
it's going to be a
minimum or maximum.

05:10.720 --> 05:12.430
And we see the intuition here.

05:12.430 --> 05:26.030
If you have-- so non-endpoint
min or max at, let's say,

05:26.030 --> 05:27.930
x is equal to a.

05:27.930 --> 05:30.660
So if you know that you have
a minimum or a maximum point,

05:30.660 --> 05:32.650
at some point x is
equal to a, and x

05:32.650 --> 05:36.399
isn't the endpoint
of some interval,

05:36.399 --> 05:37.940
this tells you
something interesting.

05:37.940 --> 05:39.356
Or at least we
have the intuition.

05:39.356 --> 05:42.710
We see that the derivative
at x is equal to a

05:42.710 --> 05:44.640
is going to be equal to 0.

05:44.640 --> 05:49.450
Or the derivative at x is equal
to a is going to be undefined.

05:52.020 --> 05:54.010
And we see that in
each of these cases.

05:54.010 --> 05:58.300
Derivative is 0, derivative
is 0, derivative is undefined.

05:58.300 --> 06:03.600
And we have a word for these
points where the derivative is

06:03.600 --> 06:06.600
either 0, or the
derivative is undefined.

06:06.600 --> 06:08.113
We called them critical points.

06:13.640 --> 06:16.130
So for the sake
of this function,

06:16.130 --> 06:25.380
the critical points are,
we could include x sub 0,

06:25.380 --> 06:27.580
we could include x sub 1.

06:27.580 --> 06:30.330
At x sub 0 and x sub
1, the derivative is 0.

06:30.330 --> 06:34.670
And x sub 2, where the
function is undefined.

06:34.670 --> 06:38.390
Now, so if we have a
non-endpoint minimum or maximum

06:38.390 --> 06:41.935
point, then it's going
to be a critical point.

06:41.935 --> 06:43.560
But can we say it
the other way around?

06:43.560 --> 06:47.120
If we find a critical point,
where the derivative is 0,

06:47.120 --> 06:48.830
or the derivative is
undefined, is that

06:48.830 --> 06:52.670
going to be a maximum
or minimum point?

06:52.670 --> 06:57.890
And to think about that, let's
imagine this point right over

06:57.890 --> 06:58.520
here.

06:58.520 --> 07:01.590
So let's call this x sub 3.

07:01.590 --> 07:03.690
If we look at the tangent
line right over here,

07:03.690 --> 07:05.390
if we look at the
slope right over here,

07:05.390 --> 07:10.300
it looks like f prime of
x sub 3 is equal to 0.

07:10.300 --> 07:12.290
So based on our definition
of critical point,

07:12.290 --> 07:16.010
x sub 3 would also
be a critical point.

07:16.010 --> 07:20.150
But it does not appear to be
a minimum or a maximum point.

07:20.150 --> 07:24.070
So a minimum or maximum
point that's not an endpoint,

07:24.070 --> 07:25.960
it's definitely going
to be a critical point.

07:25.960 --> 07:28.800
But being a critical
point by itself

07:28.800 --> 07:31.720
does not mean you're at a
minimum or maximum point.

07:31.720 --> 07:33.870
So just to be clear
that all of these points

07:33.870 --> 07:35.990
were at a minimum
or maximum point.

07:35.990 --> 07:38.527
This were at a critical
point, all of these

07:38.527 --> 07:39.360
are critical points.

07:39.360 --> 07:41.860
But this is not a
minimum or maximum point.

07:41.860 --> 07:43.810
In the next video, we'll
start to think about

07:43.810 --> 07:47.390
how you can differentiate,
or how you can tell,

07:47.390 --> 07:52.480
whether you have a minimum or
maximum at a critical point.
